{
  "result": "There should be a museum of ordinary things that almost disappeared without anyone noticing. A chipped blue mug. The rubber band around a bundle of letters. A grocery list written by someone who always remembered peaches but forgot soap. Nothing valuable enough to insure, everything valuable enough to make someone pause.\n\nThe galleries would be quiet, but not solemn. Visitors could open drawers and turn handles. Somewhere, an old refrigerator would hum with the peculiar confidence of a machine that has kept milk cold through three family arguments and a birthday party nobody wanted to end.\n\nEach object would have two labels. The first would tell you what it was. The second would tell you what it meant. \u201cKitchen chair, pine, approximately forty years old.\u201d Beneath that: \u201cThe place where he sat to peel apples in one long ribbon, which his daughter believed was a kind of magic.\u201d\n\nPerhaps the final room would be empty except for a table and a stack of cards. You would write down something you had lost: a mitten, a recipe, the exact sound of a familiar knock. Then you would leave the card behind, not as a replacement, but as a small act of witness.\n\nOutside, the world would look briefly rearranged. Your keys would feel heavier. The cracked button on your coat would seem less like a flaw. And when you got home, you might keep the ugly bowl another year, simply because it had been there for so many ordinary, irreplaceable mornings.",
